<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Kobishchanov, Y. M. (1979) Axum, ed. J. W. Michels, trans. L. T. Kapitanoff. University Park, Pennsylvania: University of Pennsylvania.</li><li>Munro-Hay, S. (1988) 'The Dating of Ezana and Frumentius', Rassegna di Studi Etiopici, 32, pp. 111–127.</li><li>Phillipson, D. W. (1998) Ancient Ethiopia. Aksum: Its Antecedents and Successors. The British Museum Press.</li><li>Phillipson, D. W. (2012) Foundations of an African civilization: Aksum &amp; the Northern Horn, 1000 BC – AD 1300. Woodbridge, Suffolk: James Currey.</li><li>Sergew Hable Sellassie (1972) Ancient and Medieval Ethiopian History to 1270. Addis Ababa: United Printers.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate, kept wide: no count survives; the estimate rests on the dense settlement of the highlands of northeastern Tigray and central Eritrea that the Aksumite sites show, and the towns of Aksum, Adulis, Matara and Qohaito.</p></section>
